As a member of the Nautilus DevOps Team, your task is to perform the following:

Take a Snapshot: Take a snapshot of the devops-rds RDS instance and name it devops-snapshot (please wait devops-rds instance to be in available state).

Restore the Snapshot: Restore the snapshot to a new RDS instance named devops-snapshot-restore.

Configure the New RDS Instance: Ensure that the new RDS instance has a class of db.t3.micro.

Verify the New RDS Instance: The new RDS instance must be in the Available state upon completion of the restoration process.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Here is a step-by-step guide to complete these tasks using the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI.

### Option 1: AWS Management Console
Step 1: Wait for devops-rds to be Available & Take a Snapshot

 - Open the AWS Management Console and navigate to Amazon RDS > Databases.
 - Locate devops-rds. Verify its status shows Available.
 - Select devops-rds, click on Actions, and select Take snapshot.
 - Set the Target DB snapshot identifier to devops-snapshot.

Click Take snapshot.

Go to Snapshots in the left sidebar and wait until devops-snapshot shows a status of Available.
------------------------------------------------------

Step 2: Restore the Snapshot to a New Instance
 
  -  Under Snapshots, select devops-snapshot.
  - Click Actions > Restore snapshot.
  - Configure the restored instance:
  - DB instance identifier: devops-snapshot-restore
  - DB instance class: Choose db.t3.micro (under Burstable classes).

Keep the remaining settings as required or default, then click Restore DB instance.
----------------------------------------------------------
Step 3: Verify the New RDS Instance

- Go back to Databases.
- Monitor devops-snapshot-restore until its status changes from Creating to Available.

